  "text": "𥕢 difficulté: Advanced High School\n\n complexities The problem requires us to find polynomials of degree 3 with real coefficients and positive roots in arithmetic progression, whose coefficients are integers.\n1. Let the roots be $a-(d/2),$ $a,$ and $a+(d/2)$, where $a$ is the middle root and $d$ is the common difference.\n2. Applying Vieta's formulas, we get:\n   - $\\text{Sum of roots } = 3a = -\\left(\n     \\text{coefficient of }x^2\\right)$\n   - $\\text{Product of roots } = a^3 = \\text{constant term}$\n3. Since the coefficients are integers, the middle root $a$ must be an integer.\n4. For the product of roots, $(a-d/2) \\cdot a \\cdot (a+d/2)$ must be an integer.\n5. A sufficient condition for integers is when $a = 2k$ for some integer $k$. Now, $a = 2k$ satisfies the condition because $(2k-d/2) \\cdot (2k) \\cdot (2k+d/2)$ is an integer whenever $d$ is an integer.\n6. Therefore, the only other requirement is for the common difference $d$ to ensure integer coefficients.\n\nFor both conditions to hold, the only non-trivial solutions arise with these integer constants, leading to the following polynomials $P(x)$:\n   - Taking $a$ as even integers and checking the product, \n   - limited solutions result in the following possible polynomials: \n  x^3 - 2 \\cdot x^2 + (-5) \\cdot x + 1.\n   - This form verifies the conditions of positivity, integer coefficients, and arithmetic progression. \n\nConclusion: \n\nThere exists exactly $\\boxed{1}$ polynomial $P(x)$ fitting these characteristics.",
